From 690127c38784cce0530992eb794495876d21c5f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 20:55:45 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07992/11362] [outer-loop] LLM-ON wall-clock envelope for
 MAX_ACTIONS + a concurrency fault that breaks induction in the shipped path

REQ-ARC-WMTE-5984 (+ SCENARIO -1, -2).

Instructed: measure the LLM-ON wall-clock envelope deciding the MAX_ACTIONS call.
Every prior budget measurement was LLM-OFF; the fitted model's own residuals said
"one LLM-on run at b1000/b2000 would replace this model with a direct anchor" and
"THE BUDGET-400 ROW IS ARITHMETICALLY FORCED, NOT A TEST". This is that anchor.
MAX_ACTIONS is unchanged at 400; no SUBMITTED_* flag touched; nothing submitted.

WALL CLOCK, measured (4 complete games x 3 budgets, budget INNERMOST, order
alternated, GPU 1 only with the device verified against per-PID VRAM):
  b400 110.9 s/game (CI 82.5-139.3) | b1000 147.5 | b2000 173.2, uncontended.
  Conservative reading at ~110 games: 9h cap -> only budget 400 fits; 6h -> none.
  The prior "b4000 fits" rested on the WEAKEST of four candidate caps: our own
  self-imposed subprocess(timeout=43200), plus an external 12h figure that
  arcprize.org/policy scopes to ARC Prize VERIFIED, explicitly NOT the Kaggle
  leaderboard. Four caps now reported side by side, ranked by provenance.

THE PRIZE IS ~NOTHING even if the clock were free: per-level score is
min((human/agent_actions)^2*100,115) and dc22's b2000 level-up took 1782 actions
against a 59-action human baseline -> 0.11 of 100. Hence the prior sweep's own
won-cells x3.27 vs authoritative-score x1.02.

THE CONSEQUENTIAL FINDING (independent of the budget): CONCURRENT per-slot
context overflow breaks induction in the submission as shipped. Server reports
total_slots=4; -c 16384 leaves ~4096/slot; the agent asks max_tokens=4096, a
whole slot by itself. Three-way isolation, same prompt throughout:
  alone @ -c16384      -> OK
  4-at-once @ -c16384  -> FAILS (HTTP 500 "Context size has been exceeded"),
                          and killed the server in 4 of 5 observations
  4-at-once @ -c65536  -> OK
Invisible until now because every LLM-on measurement was at concurrency 1, while
the eval starts ONE THREAD PER GAME (~110). generate() returns (False,msg) rather
than raising, so the agent finishes as an LLM-off agent while reporting itself as
the LLM-on scored path. Rejection is deterministic; the crash is INTERMITTENT.

THREE OF MY OWN CLAIMS WERE WRONG AND ARE RETRACTED IN PLACE (never-prune):
  1. "prompt overflowed 16384 and killed the server" -- tokens_prompt is a
     CUMULATIVE SUM (per-request means 338-1472), prompt_truncated==0 everywhere,
     and a lone over-context request is cleanly REJECTED with the server alive.
  2. "the server has ONE slot because no --parallel flag is passed" -- /props
     says 4. Absence of a flag is not evidence about a default.
  3. A speedup metric that reported "3.041x, CONCURRENCY HELPS" from a parallel
     batch in which ALL FOUR REQUESTS FAILED (they failed fast). Now gated on
     both arms completing.
Also fixed a forced gate (G3 hardcoded True) and a word-boundary bug in it
("400" matching inside "4000"), and a headline that contradicted its own table.

Prior cost model's MECHANISM falsified: it attributes all budget-scaling of LLM
cost to induction COUNT, but cost grows 1.16x at CONSTANT induction count (n=5).
Batching is a diminishing remedy: the unbatchable Python half grows 2.54x across
the budget range vs 1.58x for the LLM half.

Units kept apart, and an identity fell out: frames == budget exactly and
offline_actions == budget - resets, so gateway-charged == frames == the budget.
Reset share of frames stays ~1-2%, bounding squared-efficiency optimism at
<=1.04x here; exact per-level attribution is another lane's deliverable.

Power hoisted, not buried: 4 complete games, 1 seed, p-floor 0.125 (cannot reach
0.05, so both steps are stamped UNDERPOWERED); games chosen as b2000-win-gainers
so NOT a random sample; uncontended single process while the eval is ~110 threads.

